WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.040
Imagine a factory, like a really massive industrial chemical plant.

00:04.320 --> 00:08.060
Okay, I'm picturing it. Lots of smokestacks, heavy machinery, the works.

00:08.220 --> 00:12.660
Exactly. But this isn't your standard top-down corporation.

00:13.160 --> 00:16.180
This factory has been completely democratized.

00:16.800 --> 00:19.760
Oh, right. So the workers have absolute control.

00:19.960 --> 00:22.500
Total control. There are no billionaire shareholders.

00:23.160 --> 00:26.820
There's no distant executives sitting in a glass tower somewhere.

00:27.040 --> 00:27.460
Nice.

00:27.600 --> 00:29.320
Yeah, it sounds great, right?

00:29.320 --> 00:36.020
The workers vote on their hours, they set their own pay scales, and they collectively decide on their production targets.

00:36.740 --> 00:39.780
So it sounds like basically a perfect economic utopia.

00:40.180 --> 00:40.780
It really does.

00:41.400 --> 00:45.860
But what if I told you that exact same factory, full of these perfectly empowered workers,

00:46.480 --> 00:53.360
could democratically vote to, like, dump toxic, cancer-causing waste straight into your local drinking water?

00:53.720 --> 00:54.100
Wow.

00:54.100 --> 01:00.860
Yeah. And under a system of pure economic democracy, there is mathematically nothing you could do to stop them.

01:00.860 --> 01:10.240
I mean, that completely shatters the illusion that simply changing who gets to vote magically solves the structural consequences of, well, what actually gets built.

01:10.520 --> 01:10.840
Exactly.

01:10.840 --> 01:17.300
And that uncomfortable, like, deeply fascinating tension is exactly what we are unpacking today.

01:17.920 --> 01:20.120
So welcome to this deep dive.

01:20.380 --> 01:21.680
We're so glad you're joining us.

01:21.800 --> 01:22.000
Yeah.

01:22.000 --> 01:28.060
You are probably here because you're looking at the global economy, the housing crises, the climate headlines,

01:28.260 --> 01:33.760
and you're just trying to make sense of whether there is a fundamentally better way to run this planet.

01:34.420 --> 01:39.340
You want to cut through the political noise, right, and look at the actual architecture of how decisions get made.

01:39.460 --> 01:39.720
Right.

01:39.720 --> 01:44.160
And we have, honestly, the perfect architectural blueprint to examine today.

01:44.880 --> 01:45.840
We really do.

01:46.580 --> 01:51.320
Today we are exploring this brilliant philosophical and economic critique.

01:51.320 --> 01:58.180
It's a 2026 essay by the independent researcher Flyxion titled Authorization and Its Remainder.

01:58.960 --> 02:07.280
And what makes this piece so compelling, I think, is that it was written as a direct structural response to a very prominent long-form interview.

02:07.280 --> 02:12.160
Right. The one between the economic anthropologist Jason Hickel and Miriam Francois.

02:12.480 --> 02:21.600
Yes. And to set the stage for you, we have to look at the massive, just glaring contradiction that Jason Hickel points out in that interview.

02:22.440 --> 02:24.820
It's the reality we are all living through right now, isn't it?

02:24.880 --> 02:25.440
Oh, absolutely.

02:25.920 --> 02:31.900
He highlights that we possess unprecedented, historically mind-boggling technological capacity.

02:32.100 --> 02:34.300
Like, we can edit genes.

02:34.820 --> 02:36.380
We can land rockets backwards.

02:36.380 --> 02:38.740
We carry supercomputers in our pockets.

02:38.920 --> 02:46.940
We have an abundance of productive power that would look like literal witchcraft to someone living 100 years ago.

02:47.380 --> 02:47.860
Completely.

02:48.220 --> 02:49.240
And yet, look around.

02:49.740 --> 02:52.720
We have rampant homelessness in the wealthiest cities on Earth.

02:52.960 --> 02:57.840
We have completely inadequate, you know, crumbling public transit systems.

02:58.080 --> 02:58.280
Right.

02:58.640 --> 03:00.560
A massive lack of affordable housing.

03:00.920 --> 03:05.360
And we are just dragging our feet on the renewable energy transition.

03:05.360 --> 03:06.920
It's a profound paradox.

03:06.920 --> 03:14.180
I mean, how can a society be so capable and yet so structurally deficient in meeting basic human needs?

03:14.340 --> 03:14.740
It's wild.

03:15.300 --> 03:17.540
And Hickel's diagnosis is very specific here.

03:18.060 --> 03:30.480
He argues that this happens because the direction of our productive capacity, so our investments, our labor, our raw materials, is dictated primarily by private profit rather than by democratic will.

03:30.480 --> 03:37.320
The people with the people with the investable assets, right, the capital owners, they basically get to dictate the menu of reality.

03:37.760 --> 03:38.740
That's a good way to put it.

03:39.040 --> 03:46.440
So according to Hickel, this profit-driven engine results in an economy that wildly overproduces things with high private returns.

03:46.440 --> 03:50.900
So we get, like, endless fleets of massive gas-guzzling SUVs.

03:51.400 --> 03:55.540
We get cheap, disposable, fast fashion that just falls apart in a week.

03:55.680 --> 03:55.840
Yeah.

03:55.960 --> 04:00.900
And we get billions of dollars poured into ad tech designed to make us click on things we honestly don't even need.

04:01.080 --> 04:01.460
Exactly.

04:02.180 --> 04:10.460
But then on the flip side, that exact same system wildly underproduces things that have massive social value but low private returns.

04:10.640 --> 04:10.820
Right.

04:10.920 --> 04:12.440
Like durable public housing.

04:12.640 --> 04:15.500
Or resilient energy grids or preventative health care.

04:15.500 --> 04:20.300
So Hickel's proposed solution to this whole mess is to democratize the economy.

04:20.980 --> 04:27.520
He advocates for taking the power of authorization away from the private owners of capital and giving it to the workers and the citizens.

04:27.760 --> 04:33.400
The idea being that if the people authorize production, they will authorize production that serves human needs.

04:33.680 --> 04:35.860
Which sounds completely intuitive, right?

04:36.040 --> 04:36.280
Totally.

04:36.700 --> 04:41.180
If the current bosses are building the wrong things, just fire the bosses and let the people decide what to build.

04:41.260 --> 04:41.760
It makes sense.

04:41.760 --> 04:45.100
But this is precisely where Flyxion's essay enters the picture.

04:45.100 --> 04:55.280
And we should clarify, our goal today isn't to take a political side on capitalism versus socialism or to, you know, validate the specific empirical claims of Hickel or Flyxion.

04:55.460 --> 04:55.640
Right.

04:55.720 --> 04:56.400
We're keeping it neutral.

04:56.760 --> 04:57.080
Exactly.

04:57.080 --> 05:06.120
We are objectively mapping out Flyxion's core structural critique because Flyxion looks at Hickel's proposal and asks a really devastating structural question.

05:06.260 --> 05:06.480
Okay.

05:06.860 --> 05:07.300
Let's hear it.

05:07.540 --> 05:20.700
Even if we wave a magic wand tomorrow and completely democratize the authorization of production, even if every factory and every investment fund is run by a democratic assembly, what vital mechanisms of reality are we still completely ignoring?

05:21.040 --> 05:21.340
Okay.

05:21.500 --> 05:22.460
Let's unpack this.

05:23.000 --> 05:26.160
Because Flyxion makes this crucial distinction right out of the gate.

05:26.160 --> 05:36.900
But the power to authorize a decision, like the power to raise your hand in a vote and say, yes, let's build a public rail line, is not the same thing as the power to ensure the rail line actually gets built properly.

05:37.100 --> 05:37.280
Right.

05:37.340 --> 05:38.920
Or that it doesn't bankrupt the town.

05:39.200 --> 05:41.360
Or that it actually takes people where they need to go.

05:41.500 --> 05:41.880
Exactly.

05:42.040 --> 05:43.420
And that is the core of the argument.

05:43.880 --> 05:45.720
And it's what gives the essay its title, right?

05:45.780 --> 05:46.660
The Remainder.

05:46.960 --> 05:47.240
Yes.

05:47.500 --> 05:55.040
Flyxion argues that any institution that directs productive capacity in a society performs a combination of five distinct functions.

05:55.040 --> 05:55.980
Okay, five functions.

05:55.980 --> 06:02.940
And it doesn't matter if that institution is a Wall Street hedge fund, a socialist state ministry, or a grassroots worker cooperative.

06:03.200 --> 06:05.180
They all have to do these five things.

06:05.620 --> 06:11.200
Let's break those five functions down for everyone listening, because this really is the foundation for everything we are going to talk about today.

06:11.540 --> 06:11.740
Okay.

06:12.040 --> 06:13.620
The first function is proposal.

06:14.280 --> 06:16.280
This is the genesis of an idea.

06:16.540 --> 06:20.880
So someone somewhere puts forward a candidate allocation of resources.

06:20.880 --> 06:21.360
Right.

06:22.080 --> 06:27.460
They say, we have a thousand tons of steel, a hundred engineers, and ten million dollars.

06:27.800 --> 06:30.380
I propose we use them to build a wind farm outside the city.

06:30.900 --> 06:32.300
Someone has to pitch the idea.

06:32.600 --> 06:32.940
Exactly.

06:33.120 --> 06:35.160
They have to put a plan on the table.

06:35.720 --> 06:36.040
Exactly.

06:36.840 --> 06:39.520
Then comes the second function, which is authorization.

06:39.980 --> 06:40.220
Okay.

06:40.640 --> 06:46.100
This is the step where somebody with recognized standing looks at the proposal and formally approves it.

06:46.600 --> 06:49.880
In a capitalist firm, the board of directors authorizes it.

06:49.880 --> 06:53.920
In a state-planned economy, maybe a government bureau authorizes it.

06:54.480 --> 07:00.040
And in Hickel's proposed economic democracy, the workers or a citizens' assembly would authorize it through a vote.

07:00.180 --> 07:00.460
Right.

07:01.000 --> 07:02.520
They put their stamp of approval on it.

07:02.840 --> 07:03.660
The decision is made.

07:04.500 --> 07:05.940
The third function is execution.

07:06.500 --> 07:09.340
This is where the abstract plan actually meets the physical world.

07:09.600 --> 07:10.780
So resources are committed.

07:11.260 --> 07:12.300
Supply chains are activated.

07:12.940 --> 07:13.940
Labor is mobilized.

07:14.320 --> 07:16.320
Concrete is poured, and the project is built.

07:16.320 --> 07:19.840
So literally getting your hands dirty and building the wind farm.

07:20.000 --> 07:20.260
Yes.

07:21.120 --> 07:22.300
Fourth is evaluation.

07:22.580 --> 07:22.720
Okay.

07:22.920 --> 07:23.520
Evaluating it.

07:24.040 --> 07:28.640
Once the project is completed, or at least operating, the realized outcome has to be assessed.

07:29.200 --> 07:32.800
Did the wind farm actually generate the megawatts of power reprojected?

07:32.860 --> 07:34.340
Did it stay within the proposed budget?

07:34.920 --> 07:38.760
Did it cause unforeseen ecological damage to bird migration routes?

07:39.140 --> 07:42.180
You have to measure the reality against the original purpose.

07:42.180 --> 07:46.700
So basically checking the work, seeing if the thing you built actually does what you hoped

07:46.700 --> 07:47.120
it would do.

07:47.240 --> 07:47.580
Exactly.

07:48.120 --> 07:50.280
And finally, the fifth function is revision.

07:51.000 --> 07:54.740
Based on the results of the evaluation, the institution alters its future behavior.

07:55.120 --> 07:59.340
So maybe they decide never to use that specific turbine supplier again.

08:00.100 --> 08:02.540
Or maybe they change the environmental review process.

08:02.860 --> 08:06.520
Or they even change who has the standing to propose projects in the first place.

08:06.520 --> 08:06.920
Okay.

08:07.020 --> 08:13.560
So we have proposal, authorization, execution, evaluation, and revision.

08:13.980 --> 08:15.780
That makes perfect sense.

08:15.840 --> 08:18.120
It's a complete life cycle of an economic decision.

08:19.100 --> 08:22.460
Now, here is Flyxion's primary critique of the current debate.

08:23.180 --> 08:28.440
Almost the entirety of Hickel's interview, and honestly the vast majority of political discourse

08:28.440 --> 08:32.100
around the economy, is hyper-fixated on that second step.

08:32.400 --> 08:33.000
Authorization.

08:33.620 --> 08:33.960
Yes.

08:34.200 --> 08:34.720
Authorization.

08:34.720 --> 08:39.520
The debate is completely consumed by the question of who gets to authorize.

08:40.020 --> 08:41.460
Do the shareholders get to do it?

08:41.720 --> 08:43.080
Or do the workers get to do it?

08:43.600 --> 08:46.220
But wait, I mean, isn't that a totally fair fixation?

08:46.780 --> 08:51.340
Like, if the people currently doing the authorization are hoarding all the wealth and burning the

08:51.340 --> 08:55.960
planet, isn't taking that power away from them the most urgent step?

08:56.300 --> 08:57.860
It's certainly a compelling argument.

08:57.860 --> 09:04.000
Like, giving the vote to a thousand workers instead of one billionaire CEO just seems inherently

09:04.000 --> 09:04.400
better.

09:04.720 --> 09:08.060
In terms of wealth distribution, yes, the argument is very strong.

09:08.060 --> 09:10.460
But Flyxion's point is structural.

09:10.880 --> 09:14.820
If you only democratize authorization, you are ignoring the other four functions.

09:15.280 --> 09:17.780
Proposal, execution, evaluation, and revision.

09:18.180 --> 09:18.440
Right.

09:18.640 --> 09:21.040
Those four functions form the remainder.

09:21.620 --> 09:26.820
And you cannot simply assume that because the authorization was democratic and well-intentioned,

09:27.080 --> 09:31.040
the execution and the evaluation will automatically be accurate and just.

09:31.040 --> 09:31.760
Oh, I see.

09:32.320 --> 09:35.480
Because the Democratic Assembly can totally authorize a terrible idea.

09:36.200 --> 09:36.640
Exactly.

09:37.100 --> 09:42.640
Or they can authorize a brilliant idea, but the execution gets hopelessly botched by incompetence.

09:43.020 --> 09:43.300
Yes.

09:43.860 --> 09:47.580
But the most insidious danger actually lies in the fourth step, evaluation.

09:48.580 --> 09:53.720
Flyxion points out a massive trap here using principles from information theory, framing it

09:53.720 --> 09:55.460
as a proof about self-certification.

09:55.460 --> 09:57.980
A proof about self-certification.

09:58.480 --> 09:58.600
Yeah.

09:58.800 --> 10:01.140
And the logic is actually quite elegant in its simplicity.

10:01.800 --> 10:06.540
If the report about the success of an outcome is generated using only the original authorized

10:06.540 --> 10:12.100
plan and the records of the exact same body that authorized it, that report carries zero

10:12.100 --> 10:14.960
independent information about what actually happened in the real world.

10:14.960 --> 10:15.640
Hold on.

10:15.760 --> 10:17.560
I want to make sure I am grasping this.

10:18.120 --> 10:22.180
It's essentially the ultimate grading your own homework scenario, right?

10:22.300 --> 10:23.540
That's exactly what it is.

10:23.660 --> 10:29.100
If I am the teacher and I write the test and I also take the test and then I am the only

10:29.100 --> 10:33.880
one allowed to grade the test, of course I am going to give myself an A. I designed the

10:33.880 --> 10:38.320
entire measurement apparatus to look for the things I already decided I was good at.

10:38.440 --> 10:40.520
That is the perfect analogy.

10:40.520 --> 10:43.940
And you don't even have to assume malice or corruption here.

10:44.680 --> 10:49.640
A perfectly well-intentioned democratic committee might authorize a massive public housing project.

10:49.960 --> 10:54.980
They decide in advance that the marker of success will be number of concrete foundations

10:54.980 --> 10:58.200
poured because that's a metric they can easily track internally.

10:58.460 --> 10:59.000
Makes sense.

10:59.680 --> 11:04.540
When the year ends, they evaluate their work, look at their own ledger, see that they hit

11:04.540 --> 11:08.620
their target for poured foundations, and they declare the project a resounding success.

11:08.620 --> 11:13.180
But in the real world, maybe those foundations were poured in a flood zone.

11:13.780 --> 11:14.260
Exactly.

11:14.520 --> 11:17.660
Or maybe the concrete was mixed poorly and is already cracking.

11:18.240 --> 11:24.020
Or maybe they poured the foundations but forgot to budget for plumbing, so the houses are completely

11:24.020 --> 11:24.580
unlivable.

11:25.120 --> 11:25.640
Precisely.

11:26.040 --> 11:29.980
Their evaluation process was blind to all of that because it was a closed loop.

11:30.460 --> 11:34.200
The evaluation was entirely contained within the assumptions of the authorization.

11:34.200 --> 11:38.980
Well, this is what Fleishan means by a self-certifying loop.

11:39.100 --> 11:43.280
And what's fascinating is how Fleishan maps this onto different economic systems.

11:43.820 --> 11:44.060
How so?

11:44.580 --> 11:46.800
Well, consider a traditional capitalist firm.

11:47.040 --> 11:50.100
It uses its own internal accounting to report profit.

11:50.820 --> 11:55.840
And profit is the exact criterion by which the firm justifies its past investments and secures

11:55.840 --> 11:56.460
future funding.

11:56.760 --> 11:56.880
Right.

11:56.880 --> 12:02.540
Hickel rightly critiques this as a closed, self-serving loop that ignores negative externalities

12:02.540 --> 12:03.200
like pollution.

12:03.860 --> 12:08.740
But Fleishan points out that this exact same structural closure is available to any institution

12:08.740 --> 12:13.340
that combines authorization, execution, and evaluation without a firewall between them.

12:13.720 --> 12:14.200
Oh, wow.

12:14.760 --> 12:18.680
So a purely state-run central planning board is just as vulnerable to this.

12:18.780 --> 12:19.260
Absolutely.

12:19.260 --> 12:25.400
If a state planning authority measures its success purely by how well it fulfilled its own internally

12:25.400 --> 12:30.540
generated five-year plan, it is in the exact same structural position as the private firm

12:30.540 --> 12:31.840
reporting its quarterly profit.

12:32.360 --> 12:35.680
The state planners will always find a way to certify that the plan was met.

12:36.180 --> 12:40.980
Similarly, a public investment bank that measures its success by its own lending volume

12:40.980 --> 12:42.860
is grading its own homework.

12:43.120 --> 12:46.100
So it really doesn't matter who holds the voting paddle.

12:46.100 --> 12:50.860
If the people making the decision are the only ones evaluating the outcome, they are just

12:50.860 --> 12:51.760
flying blind.

12:52.800 --> 12:57.300
Reality is going to hit them, and they won't even have the vocabulary to understand why

12:57.300 --> 12:57.780
they failed.

12:58.280 --> 13:00.160
Which brings us to the next massive problem.

13:00.560 --> 13:06.060
If self-evaluation is a trap, we have to look much closer at the reality of who is in the

13:06.060 --> 13:07.780
room making these democratic decisions.

13:08.140 --> 13:10.640
And crucially, who is locked out of the room.

13:10.780 --> 13:11.200
Exactly.

13:11.840 --> 13:14.260
This introduces the problem of constituencies.

13:14.260 --> 13:16.300
Okay, let's explore this.

13:16.460 --> 13:21.600
Because when Hickel talks about economic democracy, he brings up some really inspiring real-world

13:21.600 --> 13:22.120
examples.

13:22.400 --> 13:22.640
He does.

13:22.820 --> 13:26.760
He talks about the Mondragón cooperatives in the Basque region of Spain.

13:27.300 --> 13:30.860
This is a massive, highly successful federation of worker cooperatives.

13:31.700 --> 13:33.180
The workers elect their own managers.

13:33.660 --> 13:36.380
They vote on the direction of the enterprise they share in the profits.

13:36.520 --> 13:41.180
Right, and they vote to set strict ratios between the highest and lowest paid workers so inequality

13:41.180 --> 13:42.520
doesn't spiral out of control.

13:42.520 --> 13:47.880
Mondragón is frequently cited as the gold standard for how workplace democracy can function at

13:47.880 --> 13:48.240
scale.

13:48.700 --> 13:52.340
And Hickel also points to another incredible example, a factory in Italy.

13:52.680 --> 13:54.400
Oh yeah, this one is wild.

13:55.160 --> 13:59.500
The owners of this factory, which manufactured automotive parts, decided to shut it down and

13:59.500 --> 14:00.380
lay everyone off.

14:00.460 --> 14:01.760
But the workers refused to leave.

14:01.860 --> 14:02.420
They just stayed.

14:03.040 --> 14:04.420
They essentially took over the plant.

14:04.420 --> 14:10.520
And through a democratic process, they analyzed their own machinery and skills, and they collectively

14:10.520 --> 14:15.580
decided to stop making car parts and pivot to building bicycle components and solar panels.

14:16.080 --> 14:17.320
Which is an incredible story.

14:17.740 --> 14:19.940
It is the exact manifestation of Hickel's premise.

14:20.320 --> 14:26.000
The workers took the authorization power away from a profit-seeking owner, and they redirected

14:26.000 --> 14:30.500
their productive capacity toward goods they deemed socially and ecologically necessary.

14:30.500 --> 14:33.780
It is a powerful narrative of empowerment.

14:34.760 --> 14:39.260
But Flyxion introduces a mathematical distinction here that completely changes how we view these

14:39.260 --> 14:39.840
institutions.

14:40.480 --> 14:40.520
Okay.

14:41.060 --> 14:44.420
We have to separate the population into two distinct sets.

14:44.820 --> 14:49.140
First, consider the set of all the people who are materially affected by the decisions

14:49.140 --> 14:49.820
of that factory.

14:50.040 --> 14:51.660
Let's call them the affected set.

14:51.820 --> 14:52.040
Okay.

14:52.300 --> 14:53.240
The affected set.

14:53.640 --> 14:55.000
Who is in that group?

14:55.000 --> 14:59.820
Well, it certainly includes the workers inside the factory, but it also includes the neighbors

14:59.820 --> 15:02.800
who live across the street and breathe the air from the smokestacks.

15:03.000 --> 15:03.100
Right.

15:03.420 --> 15:08.100
It includes the community 20 miles downstream who rely on the river into which the factory

15:08.100 --> 15:09.440
discharges its wastewater.

15:10.000 --> 15:14.380
It includes the distant consumers who will use the solar panels or the bicycle parts.

15:14.480 --> 15:14.720
Wow.

15:14.880 --> 15:15.060
Yeah.

15:15.360 --> 15:19.740
And from an ecological perspective, it includes future generations who will have to deal with

15:19.740 --> 15:22.100
the carbon emitted during the manufacturing process.

15:22.220 --> 15:23.880
So the affected set is massive.

15:23.880 --> 15:26.040
It spills way past the walls of the factory.

15:26.260 --> 15:26.580
Exactly.

15:27.180 --> 15:32.320
Now compare that vast group to the second set, the people who actually have the formal standing

15:32.320 --> 15:33.740
to authorize the decisions.

15:34.200 --> 15:35.920
Let's call them the authorizing set.

15:36.180 --> 15:36.400
Okay.

15:36.720 --> 15:40.920
In the case of Mondragon or that Italian factory, who is in the authorizing set?

15:41.580 --> 15:42.860
Well, just the workers.

15:43.040 --> 15:43.520
Precisely.

15:43.800 --> 15:44.580
The people on the payroll.

15:44.920 --> 15:45.360
Precisely.

15:45.740 --> 15:50.720
So mathematically, the authorizing set is always a drastically smaller subset of the affected

15:50.720 --> 15:51.060
set.

15:51.060 --> 15:56.060
There's a fundamental gap between who gets to decide and who has to live with the consequences.

15:56.600 --> 16:00.660
And Flyxion's point is that this constituency gap does not magically evaporate just because

16:00.660 --> 16:04.280
you replaced a capitalist board of directors with the Democratic Workers' Council.

16:04.640 --> 16:08.260
This brings me right back to my toxic factory hypothetical from the very beginning.

16:08.760 --> 16:09.300
Let's hear it.

16:09.780 --> 16:10.940
Let me play this out.

16:11.500 --> 16:14.260
Let's say I am a worker at a chemical plant.

16:15.060 --> 16:19.500
Under capitalism, my boss, the shareholder, decides to pump toxic sludge into the river to save

16:19.500 --> 16:20.940
money.

16:20.940 --> 16:22.640
I might hate it, but I don't have a vote.

16:23.080 --> 16:23.180
Right.

16:23.880 --> 16:25.160
Then the revolution comes.

16:25.720 --> 16:26.780
The shareholders are kicked out.

16:27.420 --> 16:29.460
Now my fellow workers and I own the plant.

16:29.680 --> 16:31.820
You have total democratic authorization.

16:32.400 --> 16:32.640
Right.

16:33.160 --> 16:35.020
But here is the brutal reality.

16:35.900 --> 16:40.980
My mortgage, my ability to send my kids to school, my health care, it all depends on

16:40.980 --> 16:42.780
the revenue generated by this chemical plant.

16:42.780 --> 16:43.380
Exactly.

16:44.380 --> 16:47.900
And upgrading the plant to zero emissions technology would bankrupt us and cost us all

16:47.900 --> 16:48.340
our jobs.

16:49.380 --> 16:56.020
So when the vote comes up, the Workers' Assembly democratically votes 100 to zero to keep pumping

16:56.020 --> 16:57.260
toxic sludge into the river.

16:58.100 --> 17:01.000
Procedurally, it was a flawless democratic process.

17:01.420 --> 17:02.280
Everyone had a voice.

17:02.280 --> 17:07.780
But structurally, the family living downstream, drinking the water, is still getting poisoned.

17:08.920 --> 17:12.300
Changing the boss from a guy in a suit to a committee in hard hats didn't help the

17:12.300 --> 17:16.180
downstream family at all because they aren't in the authorizing set.

17:16.720 --> 17:20.080
You have perfectly illustrated the constituency gap.

17:20.380 --> 17:25.680
The workers, even operating under perfect democratic consensus, are still imposing severe external

17:25.680 --> 17:31.020
costs, externalities, on a population that has zero standing in their voting procedure.

17:31.020 --> 17:32.460
It's a huge blind spot.

17:32.860 --> 17:36.060
And this isn't just a localized problem with a single factory in a single river.

17:36.660 --> 17:40.540
This gap scales all the way up to the structure of the global economy.

17:41.120 --> 17:42.740
Here's where it gets really interesting.

17:43.240 --> 17:44.440
How does it scale globally?

17:44.940 --> 17:49.100
Well, the sources delve into the model of Nordic social democracies, you know, countries like

17:49.100 --> 17:50.520
Sweden, Norway, Denmark.

17:51.320 --> 17:51.600
Right.

17:51.700 --> 17:55.360
In progressive economic circles, these countries are often held up as the ultimate ideal.

17:55.720 --> 17:56.140
They are.

17:56.140 --> 18:01.840
They have high wages, robust public services, free health care, strong labor unions, and

18:01.840 --> 18:03.500
relatively low inequality.

18:04.200 --> 18:07.140
It looks like a highly successful democratic settlement.

18:07.460 --> 18:08.660
The absolute dream scenario.

18:09.100 --> 18:12.220
But Hickel introduces a major critique of this model.

18:13.420 --> 18:18.980
He argues that this Nordic prosperity relies heavily on a system of unequal exchange with the

18:18.980 --> 18:19.680
global south.

18:20.480 --> 18:21.280
Unequal exchange?

18:21.400 --> 18:22.180
Meaning what?

18:22.180 --> 18:26.840
The hypothesis is that the high standard of living and the robust social safety nets in

18:26.840 --> 18:32.800
these northern European countries are fundamentally subsidized by their access to artificially cheap

18:32.800 --> 18:38.300
labor, cheap raw materials, and cheap energy extracted from countries in the global periphery.

18:38.300 --> 18:44.960
Basically saying, sure, Sweden is a great place to live, but they can only afford to pay their

18:44.960 --> 18:51.180
workers $30 an hour because someone in the Congo is mining cobalt for $2 a day, and someone

18:51.180 --> 18:53.420
in Bangladesh is sewing their clothes for pennies.

18:53.980 --> 18:54.420
Exactly.

18:55.280 --> 18:57.960
Now, Flyxion is very rigorous in analyzing this claim.

18:58.500 --> 19:03.120
The essay notes that the extent to which Nordic prosperity depends entirely on this unequal

19:03.120 --> 19:08.520
exchange versus how much of it is generated by their own high domestic productivity, technological

19:08.520 --> 19:14.860
innovation, and strong institutions is a highly debated, open, counterfactual question in economics.

19:14.860 --> 19:18.220
So there is serious academic literature on both sides.

19:18.340 --> 19:18.520
Yes.

19:18.760 --> 19:22.120
We aren't taking that as absolute, undisputed gospel.

19:22.320 --> 19:22.620
Right.

19:22.820 --> 19:24.080
We remain objective here.

19:24.640 --> 19:29.160
But Flyxion points out that structurally, if you accept the unequal exchange argument,

19:29.160 --> 19:33.920
it actually proves Flyxion's point about the constituency gap on a planetary scale.

19:34.500 --> 19:34.520
Wow.

19:34.780 --> 19:35.080
Okay.

19:35.480 --> 19:40.640
It demonstrates that the boundary of who gets to vote is the most critical variable in any democracy.

19:41.020 --> 19:42.320
Oh, I see where this is going.

19:42.660 --> 19:48.380
If a domestic democracy in the global north, say Norway, is perfectly egalitarian internally,

19:48.680 --> 19:53.020
with every citizen having an equal vote on how their sovereign wealth fund is managed,

19:53.020 --> 20:00.000
but that fund generates its returns by imposing severe environmental degradation or labor exploitation

20:00.000 --> 20:02.420
on populations in South America or Africa,

20:02.900 --> 20:07.260
then that domestic democracy is fundamentally unanswerable to the people bearing the costs

20:07.260 --> 20:08.200
of its decisions.

20:08.460 --> 20:08.840
Exactly.

20:09.400 --> 20:13.940
The people in the global south affected by the mining operations cannot vote in the Norwegian

20:13.940 --> 20:14.520
elections.

20:15.020 --> 20:21.120
The gap between the affected set and the authorizing set is literally separated by oceans and national

20:21.120 --> 20:21.520
borders.

20:21.740 --> 20:22.220
Precisely.

20:22.220 --> 20:28.300
No amount of internal domestic democratization in the wealthy country resolves that global gap.

20:28.760 --> 20:29.360
That's fascinating.

20:29.740 --> 20:34.760
And this realization that high internal satisfaction within a system doesn't mean the system is

20:34.760 --> 20:40.380
structurally sound or universally accountable brings us to a highly controversial real-world

20:40.380 --> 20:41.860
example discussed in the texts.

20:42.540 --> 20:43.480
The question of China.

20:43.840 --> 20:44.180
Yes.

20:44.760 --> 20:49.640
Okay, let's wade into this because this is where the definition of democracy gets really

20:49.640 --> 20:51.000
stretched and tested.

20:51.000 --> 20:54.060
It serves as the ultimate stress test for these theories.

20:54.780 --> 20:56.920
In his interview, Hickel ventures into the topic of China.

20:57.460 --> 21:03.280
He points to extensive survey data referencing long-term methodological studies, which show that

21:03.280 --> 21:08.180
large majorities of citizens in China consistently report feeling that they live in a democracy.

21:08.580 --> 21:08.880
Interesting.

21:08.880 --> 21:13.760
Furthermore, they report high levels of satisfaction, stating that their government serves their

21:13.760 --> 21:16.580
interests and responds effectively to popular needs.

21:16.580 --> 21:22.120
So if the local community says, we urgently need a high-speed rail line to connect us to the economic

21:22.120 --> 21:26.740
hub, the government mobilizes its resources and three years later, the train is running.

21:27.460 --> 21:28.960
That is high satisfaction.

21:29.260 --> 21:29.640
Exactly.

21:30.040 --> 21:33.240
The system exhibits an incredibly high degree of responsiveness.

21:33.240 --> 21:40.200
But Flyxion argues we have to rigorously deconstruct what the word democracy actually means operationally

21:40.200 --> 21:42.720
in this context, beyond just survey results.

21:43.060 --> 21:43.160
Right.

21:43.540 --> 21:47.260
Flyxion breaks the concept down into three distinct independent variables.

21:47.600 --> 21:48.800
Let's define them for everyone.

21:49.300 --> 21:52.020
The first variable is R, which stands for responsiveness.

21:52.760 --> 21:57.880
Does the governing system accurately sense popular needs and translate them into effective policy

21:57.880 --> 21:58.440
and action?

21:58.440 --> 22:01.420
The Chinese survey data suggests a high R.

22:02.940 --> 22:03.800
What are the other two?

22:04.340 --> 22:07.040
The second variable is C, contestation.

22:07.580 --> 22:13.260
Do citizens have the effective structural capacity to contest the decisions of the governing authority

22:13.260 --> 22:17.100
through independent channels that the authority itself does not control?

22:17.440 --> 22:23.740
So we are talking about independent labor unions that can legally strike without state permission.

22:24.180 --> 22:28.200
Or an opposition press that can investigate state failures without censorship.

22:28.440 --> 22:32.320
Or rival political factions that can organize alternative platforms.

22:32.840 --> 22:33.100
Exactly.

22:33.580 --> 22:35.760
It's the ability to safely and legally push back.

22:36.800 --> 22:39.320
And the third variable is A, accountability.

22:39.980 --> 22:44.780
Are there formal structural mechanisms by which the authorities can actually be sanctioned,

22:44.920 --> 22:49.860
removed from power, or peacefully replaced if they fail to perform or if they lose the mandate

22:49.860 --> 22:50.460
of the people?

22:50.720 --> 22:50.880
Okay.

22:51.240 --> 22:54.800
So responsiveness, contestation, and accountability.

22:55.500 --> 22:56.920
R, C, and A.

22:56.920 --> 23:02.220
Flyxion's critical insight here is that a system can score incredibly highly on R.

23:02.440 --> 23:08.240
It can be hyper-responsive to express material needs, while simultaneously scoring at the absolute

23:08.240 --> 23:10.080
bottom on C and A.

23:10.480 --> 23:10.780
Right.

23:11.320 --> 23:16.440
The survey data that Hickel cites is strong evidence of high responsiveness and satisfaction,

23:16.440 --> 23:22.440
but it cannot distinguish a system with high responsiveness and high contestation from a system with high

23:22.440 --> 23:24.520
responsiveness but no contestation.

23:25.000 --> 23:26.660
Let me try to visualize this.

23:26.880 --> 23:31.220
It's like, um, okay, imagine a very aggressive restaurant owner.

23:31.340 --> 23:31.560
Okay.

23:31.960 --> 23:36.760
The owner is constantly walking around the dining room, asking every single table how the food is.

23:36.760 --> 23:42.460
If a customer says the soup needs more salt, the owner runs to the kitchen, yells at the chef,

23:42.680 --> 23:44.560
and brings out saltier soup immediately.

23:45.180 --> 23:47.580
That is incredibly high responsiveness.

23:47.800 --> 23:48.260
High R.

23:48.540 --> 23:50.320
The system reacted to the feedback.

23:50.580 --> 23:50.920
Exactly.

23:51.360 --> 23:56.400
But then, the owner takes all these positive customer satisfaction surveys, goes on local

23:56.400 --> 24:01.040
television and declares, I have scientifically proven that we serve the greatest food in

24:01.040 --> 24:01.540
this city.

24:02.320 --> 24:03.300
The people have spoken.

24:03.820 --> 24:04.960
I feel a catch coming.

24:05.320 --> 24:07.260
But here is the massive catch.

24:07.740 --> 24:12.440
The owner used their wealth and connections to make it legally impossible for anyone else

24:12.440 --> 24:14.640
to open a competing restaurant within 50 miles.

24:15.060 --> 24:15.360
Ah.

24:15.680 --> 24:19.980
And if a customer writes a bad review in the newspaper, the owner sues the paper into bankruptcy.

24:20.680 --> 24:23.280
So there's zero contestation.

24:23.640 --> 24:24.040
Exactly.

24:24.040 --> 24:27.920
The owner has high responsiveness, but zero CNA.

24:28.620 --> 24:32.800
In that scenario, aren't we right back inside that self-certifying loop you mentioned at the

24:32.800 --> 24:33.280
very beginning?

24:33.780 --> 24:34.160
We are.

24:34.760 --> 24:39.360
Because the owner is only surveying the people who have literally no other option of where

24:39.360 --> 24:39.700
to eat.

24:40.260 --> 24:42.440
We are entirely trapped in that loop.

24:42.720 --> 24:47.680
A governing body that records the preferences of the population, interprets those preferences

24:47.680 --> 24:53.080
through its own ideological lens, executes the policy, and then generates the report

24:53.080 --> 24:58.540
on the satisfaction of those preferences is acting as its own self-certifying evaluator.

24:59.540 --> 25:04.260
Unless people have the structural freedom to organize alternatives that the system expressly

25:04.260 --> 25:09.800
does not want permitted, you do not actually know if that high satisfaction would survive

25:09.800 --> 25:11.120
true contestation.

25:11.120 --> 25:14.540
And this critique isn't just aimed in one direction, right?

25:15.340 --> 25:20.220
Fleishen applies this exact same rigorous analytical framework to Western liberal democracies, doesn't

25:20.220 --> 25:20.360
he?

25:20.680 --> 25:21.080
Absolutely.

25:21.580 --> 25:23.400
The structural critique cuts both ways.

25:24.180 --> 25:28.780
To demonstrate this, Fleishen points to a very famous 2014 study by political scientists

25:28.780 --> 25:33.140
Martin Gillins and Benjamin Page regarding policy outcomes in the United States.

25:33.680 --> 25:34.740
What did that study look at?

25:34.740 --> 25:40.660
Gillins and Page analyzed nearly 1,800 different policy issues over a span of two decades.

25:40.660 --> 25:45.280
They tracked what the average American voter wanted to happen on a specific issue, what

25:45.280 --> 25:50.280
the wealthiest top 10% of Americans wanted to happen on that same issue, and what organized

25:50.280 --> 25:51.720
elite interest groups wanted.

25:52.240 --> 25:54.740
Okay, so tracking different demographic desires.

25:55.440 --> 25:55.780
Yes.

25:56.160 --> 25:59.160
Then they looked at what actual laws were passed by Congress.

25:59.880 --> 26:00.740
And what was the result?

26:00.740 --> 26:03.900
The statistical result was staggering.

26:04.700 --> 26:08.520
They found that when the preferences of the economic elite diverged from the preferences

26:08.520 --> 26:13.760
of the average citizen, the policy outcomes overwhelmingly followed the desires of the elite.

26:14.120 --> 26:14.500
You're kidding.

26:14.920 --> 26:15.100
No.

26:15.740 --> 26:20.580
The influence of the average, median voter on actual policy adoption was essentially a flat

26:20.580 --> 26:23.460
line, statistically indistinguishable from zero.

26:24.280 --> 26:29.800
So if the majority of the country wants a policy, but the ultra-wealthy oppose it, the policy

26:29.800 --> 26:30.200
dies.

26:30.600 --> 26:30.860
Yes.

26:31.460 --> 26:36.060
And if the majority opposes a policy, but the ultra-wealthy want it, it usually becomes

26:36.060 --> 26:36.320
law.

26:36.640 --> 26:37.120
Exactly.

26:37.920 --> 26:40.100
So let's run that through Fleishen's variables.

26:41.020 --> 26:45.800
The United States formally possesses high C and A. There is robust contestation.

26:46.240 --> 26:47.660
You have a relatively free press.

26:47.800 --> 26:48.980
Independent unions exist.

26:49.360 --> 26:50.900
You can form rival political parties.

26:51.060 --> 26:52.320
And you can protest in the street.

26:52.580 --> 26:52.760
Right.

26:53.080 --> 26:54.460
And there is formal accountability.

26:55.020 --> 26:57.720
You can vote out the incumbent party every two or four years.

26:57.720 --> 27:01.320
We have all the constitutional machinery of C and A.

27:01.900 --> 27:06.680
But according to the Gillens and Page study, the system fails miserably at R.

27:07.420 --> 27:08.020
Responsiveness.

27:08.300 --> 27:08.520
Yes.

27:08.880 --> 27:13.560
The responsiveness to the actual needs and preferences of the majority is severely broken,

27:14.120 --> 27:15.700
captured by elite influence.

27:16.340 --> 27:17.720
This is a brilliant framing.

27:18.320 --> 27:23.980
So the Western systems might have high contestation and accountability, but terribly low responsiveness

27:23.980 --> 27:25.040
to the average person.

27:25.280 --> 27:25.620
Correct.

27:25.620 --> 27:30.960
Meanwhile, the Chinese system might have very high responsiveness to average material needs,

27:31.080 --> 27:34.200
but virtually non-existent contestation and accountability.

27:34.580 --> 27:36.040
That is the vital takeaway.

27:36.360 --> 27:41.620
You cannot just point to one of these variables, whether it's the constitutional right to vote

27:41.620 --> 27:46.560
in the West or the high survey satisfaction in China, and declare that true democracy has

27:46.560 --> 27:47.120
been achieved.

27:47.280 --> 27:47.500
Right.

27:47.500 --> 27:52.580
If you want a functional economic democracy that doesn't just build luxury yachts while

27:52.580 --> 27:56.920
people starve, or doesn't just blindly pour concrete for natural gas pipelines we don't

27:56.920 --> 27:59.780
need, you must have independent evaluators.

27:59.780 --> 28:06.960
You need people who have the standing, the protection, and the ability to contest the prevailing consensus.

28:07.580 --> 28:08.180
Precisely.

28:08.360 --> 28:11.900
But this naturally leads to a massive, almost psychological question.

28:12.360 --> 28:15.340
Where do these independent evaluators come from?

28:15.820 --> 28:22.300
And why does human history suggest that groups of people, even Democratic ones, seem incredibly

28:22.300 --> 28:24.860
eager to silence their own best critics?

28:24.860 --> 28:30.600
That is perhaps the most profound question in the entire essay, and it takes us deep into

28:30.600 --> 28:33.380
the psychological and structural limits of equality.

28:33.740 --> 28:34.000
Okay.

28:34.180 --> 28:34.720
Let's go there.

28:35.400 --> 28:40.180
In his interview, Hickel proposes some very aggressive structural measures to stop the

28:40.180 --> 28:41.720
concentration of power in society.

28:42.080 --> 28:43.660
He doesn't just want to tax the rich.

28:43.780 --> 28:48.080
He suggests implementing a 100% maximum income.

28:48.080 --> 28:48.840
Wow.

28:49.400 --> 28:52.280
A 100% maximum income.

28:52.840 --> 28:58.600
Essentially, a hard, absolute cap on personal wealth, alongside those strict pay ratios we

28:58.600 --> 29:03.600
discuss at Mondragon, where the CEO can only make, say, five times what the lowest paid

29:03.600 --> 29:04.340
janitor makes.

29:04.640 --> 29:06.500
The logic there is pretty clear.

29:06.900 --> 29:12.240
If nobody is legally allowed to hoard billions of dollars, then nobody can single-handedly dictate

29:12.240 --> 29:16.060
how society runs just by throwing their wallet around and buying politicians.

29:16.560 --> 29:16.820
Right.

29:16.820 --> 29:23.640
It directly addresses the concentration of financial ownership, but Flyxion argues that this purely

29:23.640 --> 29:28.560
economic view completely ignores a second, far more insidious type of concentration.

29:29.100 --> 29:29.600
Which is?

29:30.080 --> 29:34.780
Even if you eradicate the concentration of wealth, you still have to deal with the concentration

29:34.780 --> 29:35.860
of deference.

29:36.440 --> 29:36.860
Deference.

29:37.520 --> 29:40.500
Meaning, like, who we naturally listen to and respect.

29:41.000 --> 29:41.360
Yes.

29:41.560 --> 29:41.740
Yeah.

29:41.740 --> 29:43.580
Put 50 human beings in a room.

29:43.980 --> 29:48.440
Even if every single one of them has the exact same bank account balance and the exact same

29:48.440 --> 29:51.760
formal voting power, complete equality will not last a day.

29:51.760 --> 29:53.000
Yeah, that's just human nature.

29:53.900 --> 29:55.380
Hierarchies will naturally form.

29:56.060 --> 29:57.960
Someone in that room is going to be the most articulate.

29:58.560 --> 29:59.880
Someone will be the most charismatic.

30:00.660 --> 30:05.100
Someone will have an incredible memory for technical details, or simply be the most fiercely,

30:05.360 --> 30:06.900
passionately committed to a cause.

30:06.900 --> 30:11.000
And human nature dictates that the rest of the group will start gravitating toward that

30:11.000 --> 30:11.320
person.

30:11.960 --> 30:13.520
They will start deferring to their judgment.

30:14.000 --> 30:14.340
Exactly.

30:14.560 --> 30:15.580
They will listen to them.

30:15.800 --> 30:20.500
And suddenly, that person has concentrated power without owning a single share of corporate

30:20.500 --> 30:20.920
stock.

30:21.180 --> 30:21.460
Wow.

30:21.460 --> 30:26.920
How a society reacts to this natural emergence of the exceptional individual is what Flyxion

30:26.920 --> 30:32.640
explores next, taking a fascinating philosophical detour through the works of Friedrich Nietzsche.

30:33.000 --> 30:33.720
Okay, Nietzsche.

30:34.240 --> 30:35.060
Let's unpack this.

30:35.520 --> 30:38.780
Flyxion looks at this through two different lenses provided by Nietzsche.

30:39.160 --> 30:42.700
First, let's look at Nietzsche's early essay, Homer's Contest.

30:42.840 --> 30:43.080
Okay.

30:43.360 --> 30:47.560
He talks about the ancient Greeks and their formalized practice of ostracism.

30:48.060 --> 30:49.840
This wasn't just exiling criminals.

30:49.840 --> 30:55.780
Nietzsche specifically points to the Ephesians, who expelled a statesman named Hermodorus of

30:55.780 --> 30:56.280
Ephesus.

30:56.880 --> 30:59.260
If he wasn't a criminal, why did they kick him out of the city?

30:59.920 --> 31:03.500
They expelled him precisely because he was the best among them.

31:03.900 --> 31:04.560
Wait, really?

31:05.560 --> 31:05.880
Yes.

31:06.160 --> 31:08.560
He was too competent, too virtuous, too outstanding.

31:09.200 --> 31:12.380
The Greeks operated on this profound leveling principle.

31:12.880 --> 31:18.480
The foundation of their society was the contest, the pluralistic competition among relative equals.

31:18.480 --> 31:18.980
Huh.

31:18.980 --> 31:24.560
If one single person stands out as so vastly superior to everyone else that the outcome of

31:24.560 --> 31:28.260
every debate is a foregone conclusion, it isn't a contest anymore.

31:28.460 --> 31:30.400
It becomes a monarchy of competence.

31:31.100 --> 31:31.420
Wow.

31:32.000 --> 31:37.260
So to protect the democratic, competitive nature of their society, they literally had to banish

31:37.260 --> 31:37.900
their MVP.

31:38.460 --> 31:40.200
They kicked him out for being too good.

31:40.200 --> 31:41.160
Yes.

31:41.160 --> 31:42.920
That is the first lens.

31:43.420 --> 31:46.740
Leveling as a functional, structural safeguard for pluralism.

31:47.340 --> 31:52.320
But then Flyxion brings in a second, darker lens from Nietzsche's later works, specifically

31:52.320 --> 31:55.780
on the genealogy of morality, and thus spoke Zarathustra.

31:56.000 --> 31:56.260
Okay.

31:56.360 --> 31:57.200
The darker lens.

31:57.200 --> 32:00.980
This introduces Nietzsche's famous concept of ressentiment.

32:01.500 --> 32:02.100
Ressentiment.

32:02.840 --> 32:03.660
Like resentment.

32:04.360 --> 32:04.680
Exactly.

32:05.180 --> 32:10.560
It is the reactive psychological disposition of the collective herd against anyone who dares

32:10.560 --> 32:11.120
to stand out.

32:11.620 --> 32:16.480
In Zarathustra, Nietzsche paints this vivid picture of great striving individuals being

32:16.480 --> 32:20.560
constantly surrounded and bitten by the buzzing of the small, petty crowd.

32:20.740 --> 32:20.860
Right.

32:21.300 --> 32:25.340
The crowd simply cannot forgive the exceptional person for highlighting their own mediocrity.

32:26.160 --> 32:30.920
Out of pure, toxic resentment, the group works tirelessly to pull the outstanding individual

32:30.920 --> 32:33.180
down to the average to make them conform.

32:33.180 --> 32:34.580
We see this all the time.

32:35.080 --> 32:36.480
It's the tall poppy syndrome.

32:37.220 --> 32:40.580
The single flower that grows the highest gets its head chopped off by the lawnmower.

32:41.540 --> 32:45.940
Or, you know, in a modern workplace, the employee who works twice as fast and proposes

32:45.940 --> 32:48.880
brilliant new ideas doesn't get celebrated by their peers.

32:49.000 --> 32:51.660
They get hated by their peers for making everyone else look bad.

32:51.800 --> 32:52.220
Exactly.

32:53.180 --> 32:56.380
Now, here is Flyxion's brilliant synthesis of these two ideas.

32:56.880 --> 33:01.040
From the inside of a democratic assembly, you cannot easily tell the difference between

33:01.040 --> 33:01.920
these two motivations.

33:02.780 --> 33:03.340
What do you mean?

33:03.940 --> 33:08.560
When a group decides to silence or marginalize an exceptional member, are they doing it nobly,

33:08.560 --> 33:11.120
like the Greeks, to protect democratic pluralism?

33:11.400 --> 33:14.320
Or are they doing it out of petty, toxic ressentiment?

33:14.320 --> 33:14.880
Oh.

33:15.620 --> 33:17.640
The result is exactly the same either way.

33:17.700 --> 33:19.800
The exceptional person is removed from the equation.

33:20.040 --> 33:20.380
Yes.

33:21.180 --> 33:25.920
But what does this psychological human nature have to do with evaluating our economic decisions

33:25.920 --> 33:27.080
and building wind farms?

33:27.540 --> 33:29.700
It has everything to do with it.

33:30.100 --> 33:34.660
Because as Flyxion points out by drawing on John Stuart Mill's famous essay on liberty,

33:34.660 --> 33:41.320
the act of true evaluation, looking at a project and seeing its flaws, requires observers who

33:41.320 --> 33:44.880
are capable of drawing distinctions that the majority does not draw.

33:45.740 --> 33:48.600
Evaluators are, almost by definition, outsiders.

33:49.200 --> 33:50.380
They are dissenters.

33:50.720 --> 33:53.500
They possess highly specialized, obscure knowledge.

33:53.720 --> 33:58.620
Or they are idealists whose standards of success are much, much higher than what the comfortable

33:58.620 --> 33:59.920
majority is willing to attract.

34:00.520 --> 34:02.340
Ah, I see the collision course here.

34:02.340 --> 34:08.600
If a democratic group constantly levels, ostracizes, or silences its most exceptional or divergent

34:08.600 --> 34:13.540
members because they resent that concentration of deference or fear the disruption of consensus,

34:14.040 --> 34:19.220
then the society's capacity for independent evaluation collapses completely into majoritarian

34:19.220 --> 34:19.880
conformity.

34:20.220 --> 34:20.340
Wow.

34:20.900 --> 34:26.200
The only people left to evaluate the bridge we just built are the people who already vehemently

34:26.200 --> 34:29.340
agreed with the majority that authorized the bridge in the first place.

34:29.340 --> 34:34.520
So, going back to my homework analogy, if anyone in the classroom stands up and says,

34:34.940 --> 34:40.160
excuse me, I think the teacher wrote a fundamentally flawed test, the rest of the class shouts them

34:40.160 --> 34:44.520
down and throws paper at them because the class just wants to pass the test and go to recess.

34:45.500 --> 34:45.940
Exactly.

34:46.500 --> 34:48.000
How on earth do we prevent that?

34:48.340 --> 34:52.560
How do we design an economy that keeps our best critics from getting their heads chopped off

34:52.560 --> 34:53.160
by the crowd?

34:53.580 --> 34:57.620
Flyxion outlines a few specific structural institutional fixes.

34:57.620 --> 35:01.640
First, you have to design a system where deference is cheap to withdraw.

35:02.740 --> 35:03.520
Cheap to withdraw?

35:04.220 --> 35:04.460
Yes.

35:04.880 --> 35:09.680
You can willingly listen to the charismatic leader today, but the system must guarantee

35:09.680 --> 35:14.140
that you can stop listening tomorrow without suffering any social or economic penalty.

35:14.940 --> 35:18.500
Second, you make authority structurally costly to concentrate.

35:19.220 --> 35:21.400
And what about the evaluators themselves?

35:21.400 --> 35:27.120
For the evaluators, the inspectors, the critics, the auditors, you have to structurally protect

35:27.120 --> 35:29.360
them from the very group they are evaluating.

35:29.960 --> 35:33.380
How do you do that in practice if the economy is entirely democratic?

35:33.900 --> 35:37.420
You use mechanisms we already understand but need to expand.

35:38.220 --> 35:39.380
Think of the U.S. Constitution.

35:40.100 --> 35:42.240
The founders didn't just give people the right to vote.

35:42.700 --> 35:47.860
They established an independent judiciary with lifetime appointments so judges wouldn't be swayed

35:47.860 --> 35:49.440
by momentary populist anger.

35:49.440 --> 35:56.680
In this economic model, you protect evaluators through mechanisms like tenure, mandatory rotation

35:56.680 --> 36:02.020
of duties, appointing external auditors from completely different regions, or even using

36:02.020 --> 36:05.000
sortition, selection by lottery like a jury system.

36:05.380 --> 36:06.160
Oh, that makes sense.

36:06.840 --> 36:12.160
The vital key is that the evaluator's standing, their paycheck, and their livelihood cannot depend

36:12.160 --> 36:15.140
on the approval of the people whose project they are investigating.

36:15.140 --> 36:21.340
Okay, so we've established that we desperately need fiercely independent, structurally protected

36:21.340 --> 36:24.680
evaluators who aren't afraid to upset the democratic majority.

36:25.220 --> 36:25.480
Yes.

36:26.020 --> 36:29.860
But even if we perfectly protect these people, they still need a tool.

36:30.080 --> 36:34.240
They need a system, a framework, or a metric to actually evaluate against.

36:34.240 --> 36:39.900
If Hickel wants to throw out private profit entirely, what replaces it?

36:40.440 --> 36:45.040
Because the hard math of the real world, the fact that we only have so much steel and so

36:45.040 --> 36:46.940
much time, still exists.

36:47.440 --> 36:52.080
And this brings us head on into what economists call the calculation problem.

36:52.780 --> 36:53.900
The calculation problem.

36:53.900 --> 36:55.060
Now, we must be clear.

36:55.240 --> 37:00.740
Flyxion absolutely acknowledges the severe, destructive flaws of using profit as the sole

37:00.740 --> 37:01.860
measure of social value.

37:02.420 --> 37:04.540
Profit is a deeply distorted mirror.

37:05.020 --> 37:08.480
Yeah, I mean, it is distorted by massive monopolies holding market power.

37:09.280 --> 37:11.920
It's distorted by government subsidies for fossil fuels.

37:12.360 --> 37:16.900
And most fundamentally, it is distorted by the fact that purchasing power is vastly unequal.

37:17.460 --> 37:21.340
If one billionaire has the same amount of money as a million working class people,

37:21.340 --> 37:27.340
the free market will inevitably prioritize building super yachts and private space flights over

37:27.340 --> 37:32.260
building basic preventative health care clinics, simply because the yachts generate more profit.

37:32.620 --> 37:32.980
Exactly.

37:33.560 --> 37:36.320
Hickel is incredibly clear and persuasive on that point.

37:36.480 --> 37:36.780
Yes.

37:37.320 --> 37:42.500
But Flyxion introduces a counter concept, famously articulated by the Austrian economist F.A.

37:42.560 --> 37:44.880
Hayek, regarding dispersed knowledge.

37:45.580 --> 37:51.320
Even with all its horrific distortions and inequalities, the system of profit and loss does manage

37:51.320 --> 37:53.540
to register one crucial piece of information.

37:54.000 --> 37:55.780
It signals opportunity cost.

37:56.180 --> 37:57.020
Opportunity cost.

37:57.320 --> 38:02.600
It tells us, albeit very imperfectly, whether an organization is consuming more raw resources

38:02.600 --> 38:06.520
and labor than the broader society is willing to give it in exchange for its output.

38:06.840 --> 38:09.240
So profit isn't morality, but it is a signal.

38:09.680 --> 38:10.180
It's data.

38:10.500 --> 38:10.740
Yes.

38:11.440 --> 38:16.240
And simply deciding to reject profit as the guiding star of society does not remove the fundamental,

38:16.600 --> 38:17.940
physical reality of scarcity.

38:18.480 --> 38:19.500
You still have to do the math.

38:19.840 --> 38:20.120
Right.

38:20.120 --> 38:25.380
Flyxion provides a very specific, concrete scenario to illustrate why democracy alone

38:25.380 --> 38:26.480
can't solve this dilemma.

38:27.420 --> 38:28.240
Imagine a community.

38:28.820 --> 38:33.980
They have a strictly limited stock of resources, a finite amount of skilled labor, a specific

38:33.980 --> 38:37.080
tonnage of concrete and steel, and only a few heavy cranes.

38:37.620 --> 38:38.940
A closed system of resources.

38:39.780 --> 38:41.820
Now, this community desperately needs a new hospital.

38:42.040 --> 38:43.960
Their current medical facilities are failing.

38:43.960 --> 38:50.020
But they also desperately need several hundred new homes because families are living in overcrowded

38:50.020 --> 38:50.700
conditions.

38:51.180 --> 38:53.540
Both of those projects are incredibly socially necessary.

38:54.180 --> 38:58.400
You can't look at either one and say that's a frivolous waste of resources like a super yacht.

38:58.900 --> 38:59.300
Exactly.

38:59.860 --> 39:01.060
Both are morally vital.

39:01.280 --> 39:02.260
So how do they decide?

39:03.060 --> 39:08.320
Merely convening a democratic assembly and voting overwhelmingly to declare that their societal

39:08.320 --> 39:12.760
objective is to meet human needs doesn't magically multiply the concrete.

39:13.180 --> 39:13.680
No, it doesn't.

39:13.740 --> 39:17.340
You cannot just vote to have both if you only have the materials to build one.

39:17.480 --> 39:19.120
The vote doesn't change the laws of physics.

39:19.320 --> 39:19.500
Right.

39:19.960 --> 39:26.380
You need a procedure evaluation that can accurately represent complex tradeoffs, navigate uncertainty,

39:26.380 --> 39:32.180
and synthesize the vast amounts of highly specialized, localized information that is distributed across

39:32.180 --> 39:33.120
the entire economy.

39:33.400 --> 39:33.700
Wow.

39:34.240 --> 39:39.760
If you don't use market prices to calculate that tradeoff, what exactly is the mechanism

39:39.760 --> 39:40.740
you are using?

39:41.420 --> 39:42.840
Let me try an analogy here.

39:43.140 --> 39:43.380
Okay.

39:43.660 --> 39:47.060
Let's say humanity is flying a massive 747 airplane.

39:47.280 --> 39:47.880
Okay, I'm with you.

39:48.400 --> 39:53.140
Hickel is standing in the cockpit, pointing at the altimeter, which represents the profit motive.

39:53.140 --> 39:57.100
And he is saying, this instrument is fundamentally broken.

39:57.680 --> 40:03.040
It is distorted, it is miscalibrated, and it is currently steering us right into the side

40:03.040 --> 40:03.980
of a climate mountain.

40:04.520 --> 40:08.060
And he might be completely 100% correct about that.

40:08.220 --> 40:08.420
Right.

40:08.900 --> 40:13.960
But Flyxion is sitting in the co-pilot seat saying, okay, I agree the altimeter is flawed

40:13.960 --> 40:18.820
and dangerous, but you still need some instrument on this dashboard to tell you how high off the

40:18.820 --> 40:19.500
ground you are.

40:19.500 --> 40:24.980
You cannot navigate a 747 just by taking a democratic vote among the passengers in the

40:24.980 --> 40:27.420
cabin on where they feel the ground should be.

40:28.280 --> 40:32.240
That is a brilliant and terrifyingly accurate way to conceptualize it.

40:32.800 --> 40:38.660
Rejecting profit as the primary objective of society is a coherent political goal, but

40:38.660 --> 40:44.140
you still have to replace the heavy evaluative computational work that the profit mechanism

40:44.140 --> 40:45.240
currently does.

40:45.240 --> 40:50.680
And Flyxion warns about the extreme danger of what we can call the metric trap.

40:51.420 --> 40:52.580
What is the metric trap?

40:53.460 --> 40:58.780
It's the reality that any new metric you design to replace profit can also be gamed, corrupted,

40:59.140 --> 41:02.280
or diverge wildly from its actual intended purpose.

41:02.940 --> 41:08.280
Let's look at our current favorite metric, gross domestic product, or gross output.

41:08.580 --> 41:09.020
GDP.

41:09.020 --> 41:12.860
GDP rises when you build a beautiful new library.

41:13.360 --> 41:17.820
But GDP also rises when a massive hurricane destroys a city and you have to spend billions

41:17.820 --> 41:19.620
of dollars clearing debris and rebuilding.

41:20.080 --> 41:20.540
Oh, right.

41:21.000 --> 41:25.740
It even rises if you just demolish a perfectly functional building for no reason and rebuild

41:25.740 --> 41:26.960
it exactly the same way.

41:27.700 --> 41:29.380
It doesn't measure human flourishing.

41:29.580 --> 41:32.640
It just proves that money changed hands and activity happened.

41:32.740 --> 41:35.700
So let's apply this to our post-capitalist democratic assembly.

41:35.700 --> 41:38.300
Let's say they realize they need a metric.

41:38.820 --> 41:42.660
So they replace profit with a new democratically chosen target.

41:43.080 --> 41:44.240
Housing units completed.

41:44.680 --> 41:46.860
They want to heavily incentivize the creation of homes.

41:47.540 --> 41:49.920
Sounds like a noble, socially beneficial metric.

41:50.720 --> 41:51.920
But what happens in reality?

41:52.780 --> 41:56.220
The metric of housing units completed can skyrocket.

41:56.600 --> 41:58.380
The graph goes up, everyone cheers.

41:58.740 --> 41:59.160
But?

41:59.160 --> 42:05.300
But meanwhile, the actual quality, the safety, the geographic location, and the durability

42:05.300 --> 42:07.680
of those houses completely plummets.

42:08.260 --> 42:13.220
The builders eager to hit their democratic quotas just start throwing up cheap, tiny, uninsulated

42:13.220 --> 42:14.480
shacks in the middle of nowhere.

42:15.020 --> 42:20.360
A metric chosen democratically is still just a metric, and it is vulnerable to all the exact

42:20.360 --> 42:22.480
same gaming and distortions as profit.

42:22.480 --> 42:30.000
So, if making decisions without proper, rigorous, reality-based metrics can lead to terrible, irreversible

42:30.000 --> 42:34.320
mistakes, where does that leave us in terms of the actual physical planet?

42:34.980 --> 42:40.280
Because that brings us to the real environmental core of this whole deep dive, the concept of

42:40.280 --> 42:43.660
degrowth and the unstoppable one-way flow of time.

42:43.660 --> 42:47.560
This is where the philosophical debate hits the physical ground.

42:47.960 --> 42:53.440
Hickel's ecological argument for degrowth is arguably his most forceful and urgent point.

42:53.720 --> 42:57.840
And we should be clear here, because Flyxion notes that Hickel's specific definition of

42:57.840 --> 43:03.820
degrowth is much more careful and nuanced than how it is often caricatured in popular media.

43:03.960 --> 43:04.480
Exactly.

43:05.140 --> 43:09.080
He isn't saying we all need to go back to living in caves and wearing burlap sacks.

43:09.080 --> 43:13.600
He's not advocating for primitivism, and he's not saying developing nations in the

43:13.600 --> 43:16.540
global south shouldn't grow their economies to meet basic needs.

43:16.840 --> 43:16.920
Right.

43:17.680 --> 43:22.000
Hickel is specifically advocating for a planned, selective reduction of damaging,

43:22.300 --> 43:27.660
unnecessary production, primarily in rich, high-consumption countries, while simultaneously

43:27.660 --> 43:32.540
expanding necessary production like public transit, renewable energy grids, and health care.

43:32.940 --> 43:34.420
It is selective contraction.

43:34.700 --> 43:36.440
Shrink the bad, grow the good.

43:36.880 --> 43:38.160
Which sounds entirely reasonable.

43:38.160 --> 43:42.160
But Flyxion immediately raises the classification problem.

43:42.420 --> 43:43.340
Classification problem.

43:43.640 --> 43:51.020
How exactly do we objectively define and classify what is necessary versus what is damaging?

43:51.560 --> 43:53.940
Context is absolutely everything.

43:54.460 --> 43:54.740
Right.

43:55.160 --> 43:57.500
Take a massive, energy-hungry data center.

43:57.940 --> 44:00.340
Is that data center inherently bad?

44:00.340 --> 44:07.340
If it is burning millions of watts of energy just to run manipulative, algorithmic advertising

44:07.340 --> 44:11.120
to sell us plastic junk we don't need, then yes, it's damaging.

44:11.680 --> 44:14.200
But it's just a building full of servers.

44:14.860 --> 44:16.760
The servers themselves don't have a moral compass.

44:16.760 --> 44:18.080
Precisely.

44:18.080 --> 44:22.840
Because what if that exact same data center is dedicating all of its processing power to

44:22.840 --> 44:28.280
running complex meteorological models to predict devastating climate disasters or simulating

44:28.280 --> 44:29.940
protein folding to cure a disease?

44:30.360 --> 44:33.160
Suddenly, it is an absolute necessity.

44:33.160 --> 44:33.880
Exactly.

44:34.240 --> 44:37.000
Or take the example of a large, gas-powered SUV.

44:37.680 --> 44:42.200
In a dense, urban city like London or New York with extensive public transit, that SUV

44:42.200 --> 44:45.800
might be a totally unnecessary, polluting luxury item.

44:45.800 --> 44:51.520
But for a family living in a remote rural community with harsh winters, unplowed dirt roads, and

44:51.520 --> 44:56.100
a 50-mile drive to the nearest hospital, that exact same SUV isn't a luxury.

44:56.340 --> 44:58.840
It is an absolute practical lifeline.

44:59.660 --> 45:04.920
So you cannot just write a blanket overarching law from a central assembly that declares SUVs

45:04.920 --> 45:09.800
are illegal or data centers must be shut down without severely disenfranchising someone

45:09.800 --> 45:13.020
whose specific local context makes that item necessary.

45:13.560 --> 45:14.300
Man, that's tough.

45:14.300 --> 45:20.440
And this classification problem is massively compounded by the issue of foreclosure and

45:20.440 --> 45:21.520
the direction of time.

45:21.780 --> 45:22.860
Foreclosure of time.

45:23.440 --> 45:24.260
Let's dig into that.

45:24.500 --> 45:27.240
This is a critical concept for understanding physical reality.

45:28.160 --> 45:32.760
Decisions about production made in the present moment create rigid, physical constraints on

45:32.760 --> 45:33.120
the future.

45:33.760 --> 45:35.440
Think of time as a narrowing hallway.

45:35.660 --> 45:35.900
Okay.

45:36.220 --> 45:37.160
A narrowing hallway.

45:37.660 --> 45:43.220
Every door you choose to walk through today permanently locks three other doors behind you for tomorrow.

45:43.220 --> 45:49.060
So the choices we make today drastically shrink the menu of choices we have available to us in the future.

45:49.500 --> 45:50.000
Exactly.

45:50.500 --> 45:55.520
If a democratic assembly votes today to build an entire national infrastructure around a specific

45:55.520 --> 46:00.000
technology, let's say they decide to heavily invest in natural gas pipelines because it's a

46:00.000 --> 46:00.960
bridge fuel.

46:00.960 --> 46:08.240
They pour millions of tons of concrete, lay thousands of miles of steel pipe, and train tens of thousands of workers in that specific industry.

46:08.240 --> 46:13.000
And once that is done, they have locked themselves into that specific supply chain.

46:13.080 --> 46:13.260
Yes.

46:13.940 --> 46:19.560
They have functionally bankrupted their ability to easily switch to solar or geothermal energy next year

46:19.560 --> 46:23.860
because all the capital, material, and labor is sunk into the gas infrastructure.

46:24.140 --> 46:25.940
They foreclose their future options.

46:25.940 --> 46:31.400
An allocation procedure that only looks at present benefits, whether it's looking at present financial

46:31.400 --> 46:37.600
profit or present democratically expressed popular preferences, can severely shrink the set of future

46:37.600 --> 46:39.860
options without anyone ever intending it.

46:40.340 --> 46:43.360
We trap our future selves in a cage built by our past decisions.

46:44.020 --> 46:50.100
Therefore, Flyxion argues, a truly resilient democratic society must aggressively preserve the ability to

46:50.100 --> 46:51.380
reverse its own mistakes.

46:51.780 --> 46:52.060
Right.

46:52.060 --> 46:57.880
A transition that is authorized today by a majority vote, but is discovered to be a catastrophic mistake

46:57.880 --> 47:02.360
tomorrow by independent evaluators, must remain structurally correctable.

47:02.860 --> 47:07.480
Which means we can't build massive, sprawling, globalized systems that are impossible to undo.

47:07.880 --> 47:08.300
Exactly.

47:08.520 --> 47:13.640
And to manage this, to ensure that the people who are affected by a mistake are the ones who have the power to fix it,

47:14.240 --> 47:18.500
Flyxion introduces a fascinating, radical solution based on locality.

47:18.500 --> 47:22.260
It is a way to finally close that constituency gap.

47:22.440 --> 47:23.500
Shrinking the distance.

47:23.760 --> 47:24.020
Yes.

47:24.680 --> 47:29.380
Remember that gap between the authorizing set, the people who decide, and the affected set,

47:29.520 --> 47:30.920
the people who suffer the consequences?

47:31.040 --> 47:31.940
Yeah, we talked about that earlier.

47:32.420 --> 47:35.500
Geographic distance is the primary generator of that gap.

47:36.080 --> 47:40.920
Under our current hyper-globalized system, the extraction of raw materials happens in one region,

47:41.240 --> 47:42.920
say, mining lithium in South America.

47:42.920 --> 47:47.820
The assembly happens in a second region, a massive factory complex in East Asia.

47:48.440 --> 47:52.860
The product is consumed and enjoyed in a third region, a suburban home in North America.

47:53.460 --> 47:57.560
And finally, it is disposed of as toxic electronic waste in a fourth region,

47:58.120 --> 47:59.740
shipped off to a landfill in West Africa.

48:00.020 --> 48:02.940
The costs are completely decoupled from the benefits.

48:03.460 --> 48:08.200
The person voting with their wallet or their ballot in North America has absolutely no sensory

48:08.200 --> 48:12.600
or political connection to what that product is doing to the groundwater in South America

48:12.600 --> 48:14.140
or the soil in Africa.

48:14.620 --> 48:14.800
Exactly.

48:15.640 --> 48:21.400
So the limit case solution proposed here by Flyxion is a radical, physical restructuring

48:21.400 --> 48:22.940
of how we make everything.

48:23.860 --> 48:25.060
The guiding principle is this.

48:25.800 --> 48:28.360
Information moves and matter stays.

48:28.840 --> 48:30.460
Information moves, matter stays.

48:30.580 --> 48:32.860
What does that actually look like in practice for a community?

48:32.860 --> 48:37.680
It means that instead of manufacturing millions of physical, plastic, and metal objects in

48:37.680 --> 48:43.420
one megafactory and shipping them across the ocean on massive bunker fuel-burning cargo

48:43.420 --> 48:46.960
ships, we circulate the designs globally over the internet.

48:47.320 --> 48:47.680
Ah.

48:48.100 --> 48:53.420
The instructions, the CAD files, the software blueprints, the engineering schematics, those

48:53.420 --> 48:56.000
move instantly and weightlessly across the globe.

48:56.220 --> 49:01.460
But the actual physical fabrication of the object happens locally within the community where

49:01.460 --> 49:02.680
it will actually be used.

49:02.860 --> 49:06.780
So a medium-sized town doesn't import tractors from overseas.

49:06.780 --> 49:13.260
They have a local, high-tech fabrication hub equipped with advanced 3D printers, CNC machining,

49:13.580 --> 49:14.320
automated looms.

49:14.560 --> 49:19.220
They download the open-source design for a tractor, and they physically build the tractor

49:19.220 --> 49:20.380
right there in their own town.

49:20.600 --> 49:20.880
Yes.

49:21.440 --> 49:25.380
Now, Flyxion is realistic and acknowledges the physical limits of this vision.

49:25.940 --> 49:30.440
You cannot 3D print a cutting-edge, 3-nanometer semiconductor in your garage.

49:30.440 --> 49:36.160
That requires incredibly complex, globalized, dust-free clean rooms and supply chains.

49:36.520 --> 49:39.680
You also cannot grow coffee beans in a freezing Nordic climate.

49:40.240 --> 49:40.440
Right.

49:40.620 --> 49:42.240
Physics and geography still apply.

49:42.240 --> 49:43.920
But the principle remains.

49:44.400 --> 49:50.040
Moving as much production as physically possible to local hubs drastically shrinks the constituency

49:50.040 --> 49:50.620
gap.

49:51.020 --> 49:56.080
If you mine the materials locally, build the product locally, use it locally, and dispose

49:56.080 --> 50:00.920
of it locally, the people making the democratic decisions and the people living with the environmental

50:00.920 --> 50:04.740
consequences are finally the exact same group of people.

50:04.740 --> 50:10.460
You know, there is a fascinating hypothesis mentioned in the text about human travel that

50:10.460 --> 50:13.560
connects directly to this idea of local production.

50:13.800 --> 50:14.840
It really stuck with me.

50:15.160 --> 50:15.320
Oh.

50:15.720 --> 50:16.280
What is it?

50:16.700 --> 50:18.720
The text asks a simple question.

50:19.300 --> 50:24.560
Why are consumer flights continuing to climb year after year, even as everyone is increasingly

50:24.560 --> 50:26.660
aware of the ecological damage they cause?

50:26.900 --> 50:28.220
That's a great behavioral question.

50:28.800 --> 50:30.360
Why do we keep flying so much?

50:30.360 --> 50:36.080
The hypothesis is that we are constantly flying around the world just to escape our own opaque,

50:36.440 --> 50:38.400
finished, mundane local environments.

50:38.740 --> 50:43.300
Because we don't make anything where we live anymore, our towns and suburbs feel like closed

50:43.300 --> 50:48.500
boxes where everything just magically arrives on our doorstep from a distant Amazon warehouse.

50:48.740 --> 50:48.840
Wow.

50:49.040 --> 50:49.240
Yeah.

50:49.480 --> 50:55.180
The text suggests that if our local towns were actually places of open, legible production,

50:55.920 --> 51:00.180
places where we could walk down the street and literally see and participate in how our

51:00.180 --> 51:05.900
clothes, our tools, our vehicles, and our infrastructure are made, would we feel less of this desperate

51:05.900 --> 51:09.100
psychological need to escape on a plane to somewhere else?

51:09.480 --> 51:12.060
It is a profound, albeit speculative, thought.

51:12.740 --> 51:15.860
It suggests that alienation isn't just about not owning the factory.

51:16.620 --> 51:19.420
It's about being physically severed from the act of creation.

51:20.760 --> 51:25.360
An environment where the making of things is open to inspection and participation is one

51:25.360 --> 51:28.400
where the daily world is far less opaque and alienating.

51:28.400 --> 51:31.740
But that brings up a really urgent, practical concern.

51:31.920 --> 51:37.660
If we are making all these things locally, constantly downloading designs and fabricating

51:37.660 --> 51:42.660
blenders and tools and clothes, how do we make sure we aren't just creating localized

51:42.660 --> 51:43.940
mountains of trash?

51:44.520 --> 51:44.740
Right.

51:44.900 --> 51:48.800
If I can just print a new blender every time mine breaks, my town is going to be buried in

51:48.800 --> 51:50.500
broken plastic blenders in a week.

51:50.500 --> 51:56.940
And that leads directly to the final and perhaps most mind-bending physical solution proposed by

51:56.940 --> 51:57.300
Flyxion.

51:57.760 --> 52:03.100
It is a complete structural redefinition of what garbage actually is.

52:03.760 --> 52:05.440
The end of garbage.

52:05.880 --> 52:06.080
Yes.

52:06.620 --> 52:10.900
Flyxion defines garbage strictly through the lens of accounting, not just physical decay.

52:11.500 --> 52:16.180
An object becomes garbage at the exact moment it leaves every ledger in society.

52:16.680 --> 52:18.580
What do you mean by leaving a ledger?

52:18.580 --> 52:25.060
When a massive corporation manufactures a smartphone, that phone exists on their ledger as inventory.

52:25.720 --> 52:26.360
It has an owner.

52:26.920 --> 52:29.360
When they sell it to you, their account closes.

52:29.820 --> 52:31.740
The phone moves to your personal ledger.

52:32.020 --> 52:33.020
You are now the custodian.

52:33.300 --> 52:33.440
Okay.

52:33.980 --> 52:37.860
But two years later, when the screen cracks and the battery dies and you throw it in the

52:37.860 --> 52:39.920
trash can, your account closes.

52:40.580 --> 52:46.020
At that precise moment, the object has no owner, no custodian, and no one is responsible

52:46.020 --> 52:46.440
for it.

52:46.440 --> 52:48.240
It has fallen off every ledger.

52:49.040 --> 52:49.680
I see.

52:50.180 --> 52:53.520
That is the moment it conceptually and legally becomes garbage.

52:54.220 --> 52:59.000
And all the toxic costs of the heavy metals inside that phone are immediately pushed onto

52:59.000 --> 53:00.420
the environment and the public.

53:00.420 --> 53:05.820
So the solution isn't just to put a green recycling bin on the corner and hope for the best?

53:06.460 --> 53:11.420
No, because traditional recycling often just dissipates energy and material, and it relies

53:11.420 --> 53:13.880
on someone voluntarily taking responsibility.

53:14.880 --> 53:20.480
The structural solution is to design an economy where no physical object ever leaves the ledger.

53:21.380 --> 53:25.160
Flyxion outlines three strict physical requirements for the manufacturing of the future.

53:25.160 --> 53:27.660
Okay, let's hear these requirements.

53:27.660 --> 53:33.820
First, every single manufactured object must be capable of being cleanly disassembled by

53:33.820 --> 53:36.360
the receiver using commonly available means.

53:37.100 --> 53:41.680
No more electronics that are glued shut with proprietary adhesives that force you to shatter

53:41.680 --> 53:43.100
the glass just to change a battery.

53:43.340 --> 53:43.480
Right.

53:43.480 --> 53:54.260
Second, every object must be returned to a named, specific custodian when it no longer

53:54.260 --> 53:55.500
serves its primary purpose.

53:56.000 --> 54:01.000
There is no concept of throwing it away because there is no geographical or legal away.

54:01.460 --> 54:04.400
It goes back to a designated accountable materials account.

54:04.400 --> 54:06.360
And the third requirement?

54:07.120 --> 54:11.060
This is the most crucial one for our entire discussion about independent evaluation.

54:11.860 --> 54:18.780
Third, every physical object must carry its own inherently testable record of its materials,

54:19.160 --> 54:21.300
its components, and its assembly sequence.

54:22.000 --> 54:22.440
Okay, wait.

54:22.940 --> 54:26.780
We talked extensively earlier about the trap of self-certifying loops.

54:27.020 --> 54:31.300
If the company that makes the blender just prints a nice glossy paper label and sticks it on

54:31.300 --> 54:37.140
the box that says, made of 100% ethically sourced good stuff, isn't that just a self-report?

54:37.340 --> 54:37.760
Exactly.

54:38.040 --> 54:40.500
Why should an independent evaluator trust the label?

54:40.840 --> 54:44.200
A label written by the producer is just a claim, a PR statement.

54:45.020 --> 54:49.420
A society built on corporate labels is a society of uninformative self-reports.

54:50.280 --> 54:55.480
The value of this record only exists if it can be physically tested against the object itself

54:55.480 --> 54:58.100
by an independent third-party evaluator.

54:58.280 --> 54:58.480
Right.

54:58.480 --> 55:03.780
If a device has a label that says, completely repairable, but the evaluator tries to open

55:03.780 --> 55:09.280
it and finds it welded shut, or if it says, contains only copper, but you open it and

55:09.280 --> 55:14.700
find toxic lead, the discrepancy is immediately, physically obvious to anyone who tests it.

55:15.140 --> 55:18.820
So how do we ensure the record doesn't just get separated from the object?

55:19.020 --> 55:22.620
Like, I lose the instruction manual or the paper sticker peels off in the rain.

55:22.860 --> 55:24.680
How does the object keep its ledger?

55:24.680 --> 55:29.500
Flyxion proposes embedding the information directly into the physical structure of the

55:29.500 --> 55:31.620
object itself, at a microscopic level.

55:32.040 --> 55:37.540
We are talking about utilizing technologies like laser-etched holographic patterns written

55:37.540 --> 55:43.760
directly into the surface of stone, brick, wood, cloth, or deeply embedded into the molecular

55:43.760 --> 55:46.020
structure of a 3D-printed polymer object.

55:46.020 --> 55:47.660
Whoa.

55:47.660 --> 55:52.340
So the information is physically scarred into the material itself?

55:52.800 --> 55:57.060
The exact engineering parameters are still open to innovation, but the core principle

55:57.060 --> 55:58.420
is what matters structurally.

55:59.220 --> 56:01.680
The record travels permanently with the object.

56:02.280 --> 56:06.780
It remains legible and readable long after the object has been separated from the factory

56:06.780 --> 56:10.000
that made it or the paperwork that originally accompanied it.

56:10.000 --> 56:15.060
It's like every single physical object from a coffee cup to a steel I-beam becomes its

56:15.060 --> 56:17.020
own black box flight recorder.

56:17.340 --> 56:17.400
Yes.

56:17.740 --> 56:21.300
But crucially, the data isn't just a PR sticker put on by marketing.

56:21.520 --> 56:25.800
Because I have the physical power to take the object apart, which was requirement one,

56:26.120 --> 56:29.200
I can independently prove if the manufacturer lied to me.

56:29.620 --> 56:33.420
The physical object itself becomes the independent evaluator.

56:33.780 --> 56:34.760
That is exactly right.

56:34.760 --> 56:42.180
It is a brilliant synthesis, the object is the evidence, the etching is the claim, and

56:42.180 --> 56:45.280
independent evaluation is simply the act of comparing the two.

56:45.680 --> 56:46.060
Incredible.

56:46.860 --> 56:51.160
Now this concept of physical legibility extends beyond just the objects.

56:51.720 --> 56:53.500
It extends to the factories themselves.

56:54.800 --> 57:00.000
Flyxion uses an incredibly evocative analogy from an old 1990s video game called Descent,

57:00.300 --> 57:03.580
which featured a revolutionary 3D cutaway auto map.

57:03.580 --> 57:05.540
Oh, I remember that.

57:06.320 --> 57:09.940
You could pause the game, spin the map around, and see all the tunnels, the rooms, and how

57:09.940 --> 57:11.620
everything intersected inside the mine.

57:11.800 --> 57:13.740
You could see the entire structure at once.

57:13.980 --> 57:14.100
Right.

57:14.520 --> 57:19.180
Flyxion argues that our physical economy, our factories, and infrastructure should be

57:19.180 --> 57:21.200
as legible as that auto map.

57:21.820 --> 57:26.920
We need movement through a physical structure where its layers, its inputs, and its outputs

57:26.920 --> 57:28.380
remain visible to the public.

57:28.960 --> 57:29.780
I love that idea.

57:29.780 --> 57:35.500
Imagine a world where local school field trips routinely conduct tours through the actual

57:35.500 --> 57:41.180
working interiors of local factories, not just a sanitized, quiet PR room with video

57:41.180 --> 57:47.320
playing, but safe, transparent viewing galleries overlooking the real, messy chemical processes.

57:47.320 --> 57:53.340
So the community can actually see, hear, and understand what is being made in their own backyard.

57:53.600 --> 57:55.340
The factory becomes a glass house.

57:55.780 --> 57:56.120
Exactly.

57:56.880 --> 57:58.180
It demystifies production.

57:58.700 --> 58:00.880
And more importantly, it trains observers.

58:01.660 --> 58:06.380
It gives the neighbors, the citizens, the actual standing, and the technical knowledge

58:06.380 --> 58:07.760
to evaluate production.

58:07.760 --> 58:12.540
If you can walk through the factory and see how a thing is made, and you can take the

58:12.540 --> 58:16.880
object apart in your living room when it's done, you are fundamentally better placed to

58:16.880 --> 58:21.080
participate in the Democratic Assembly and say whether that thing should be made in the

58:21.080 --> 58:21.580
first place.

58:22.480 --> 58:25.680
It bridges that massive constituency gap we talked about earlier.

58:25.980 --> 58:26.340
It does.

58:26.860 --> 58:30.440
It merges the affected set with the authorizing set through transparency.

58:30.440 --> 58:36.260
And to conclude this entire structural argument, Flyxion ties all of this back to the historical

58:36.260 --> 58:37.900
point that Hickel originally made.

58:38.560 --> 58:42.340
Hickel rightly credits organized labor movements for winning massive victories.

58:42.860 --> 58:47.080
The weekend, public sanitation, workplace safety, and labor rights.

58:47.740 --> 58:51.980
But Flyxion points out that these historical victories were never just simple transfers of

58:51.980 --> 58:54.860
voting authority from the bosses to the workers.

58:55.440 --> 58:58.380
What were they then, if they weren't just about getting a vote?

58:58.380 --> 59:02.740
They were about the creation of independent institutional checking mechanisms.

59:03.560 --> 59:05.960
When the labor movement fought, they didn't just win a vote.

59:06.120 --> 59:06.940
They built courts.

59:07.180 --> 59:08.420
They built a free press.

59:08.940 --> 59:10.160
They built independent unions.

59:10.160 --> 59:14.580
And they built state safety inspectorates who could walk into a factory and shut it down.

59:15.160 --> 59:15.880
They built the remainder.

59:16.240 --> 59:16.660
Exactly.

59:16.920 --> 59:21.500
And what Flyxion is arguing is that a ledger etched into the physical reality of an object,

59:21.960 --> 59:27.160
combined with a factory open to the public gaze, is the ultimate modern inspectorate.

59:27.160 --> 59:32.620
This has been an absolutely massive, mind-expanding journey today.

59:33.180 --> 59:35.820
For you listening, let's summarize the terrain we've covered.

59:36.000 --> 59:36.380
Good idea.

59:36.380 --> 59:42.140
We started with this deeply appealing, intuitive idea that simply democratizing our economy,

59:42.620 --> 59:45.560
firing the billionaires, and giving everyone a vote on what gets made,

59:46.160 --> 59:48.880
would solve our crises of resources and inequality.

59:49.620 --> 59:52.660
But through Flyxion's rigorous structural lens,

59:53.220 --> 59:57.120
we discovered that authorization is basically meaningless without its remainder.

59:57.120 --> 01:00:02.420
We discovered that we desperately need independent evaluators who are structurally protected

01:00:02.420 --> 01:00:04.060
so they aren't afraid of the crowd.

01:00:04.740 --> 01:00:09.460
We need localized production to ensure the people making the choices are the ones living with the consequences.

01:00:10.280 --> 01:00:14.660
We need robust, reality-based metrics that actually account for physical scarcity.

01:00:15.480 --> 01:00:18.620
And we need a physical reality where objects carry their own truth

01:00:18.620 --> 01:00:21.680
and refuse to fall off the ledger and become hidden garbage.

01:00:21.680 --> 01:00:24.360
And this matters to you directly.

01:00:25.080 --> 01:00:25.980
It matters today.

01:00:26.540 --> 01:00:29.940
Whether you are navigating the politics of a corporate hierarchy at your job,

01:00:30.020 --> 01:00:32.960
or you are looking at the zoning decisions of your local city council,

01:00:33.240 --> 01:00:36.900
or you are simply standing in your kitchen deciding how to take out the trash,

01:00:37.160 --> 01:00:42.820
the fundamental question you have to ask is not just who is in charge or who got the most votes.

01:00:42.820 --> 01:00:50.820
The real question, the structural question, is who has the power, the protection, and the evidence

01:00:50.820 --> 01:00:53.520
to prove the person in charge is wrong?

01:00:53.680 --> 01:00:55.940
That is the essence of true accountability.

01:00:56.420 --> 01:00:59.700
Without it, democracy is just a different flavor of blindness.

01:01:00.100 --> 01:01:05.160
I want to leave you with one final provocative thought to mull over on your own.

01:01:05.620 --> 01:01:07.560
It's inspired by everything we've discussed today,

01:01:07.660 --> 01:01:10.040
but it takes it one step further into the future.

01:01:10.040 --> 01:01:14.420
The text points out that past labor movements secured their gains by building these

01:01:14.420 --> 01:01:20.860
independent human institutions, like a free press, courts, and human inspectors to check power.

01:01:21.280 --> 01:01:21.400
Right.

01:01:21.820 --> 01:01:24.940
But imagine this proposed future we just outlined.

01:01:25.360 --> 01:01:28.580
If the physical environment becomes perfectly legible,

01:01:29.020 --> 01:01:34.100
if every single object you touch holds a laser-edged, incorruptible ledger of its own history,

01:01:34.600 --> 01:01:38.240
and every factory is totally transparent like a glass house,

01:01:38.240 --> 01:01:43.180
do we even need those traditional, fallible human institutions anymore?

01:01:43.500 --> 01:01:44.060
Wow.

01:01:44.400 --> 01:01:50.520
Or does the physical world itself become our ultimate, infallible journalist and judge,

01:01:50.980 --> 01:01:54.220
fundamentally changing what it means to hold a society accountable?

01:01:54.620 --> 01:01:58.100
It raises profound questions about the future of governance itself

01:01:58.100 --> 01:02:00.480
and whether physics can replace bureaucracy.

01:02:00.900 --> 01:02:03.540
Think about that chemical factory we talked about at the very beginning.

01:02:03.540 --> 01:02:08.300
Maybe the goal isn't just to democratize who gets to vote on running the machines.

01:02:08.980 --> 01:02:13.220
Maybe the real goal is to build a factory and an economy that can tell you,

01:02:13.460 --> 01:02:15.360
in its own physical, structural language,

01:02:15.740 --> 01:02:19.420
exactly when and why it's going to poison the river,

01:02:20.040 --> 01:02:21.320
before the vote even happens.

01:02:21.840 --> 01:02:22.220
Exactly.

01:02:22.760 --> 01:02:27.900
Thank you so much for joining us on this custom-tailored deep dive into the architecture of our economy.

01:02:28.320 --> 01:02:30.160
Keep questioning the structures around you,

01:02:30.160 --> 01:02:32.840
keep looking for the remainder, and we will catch you next time.

